{0 Generating notes: a worked example}

A small vault, three templates, and the [dune] rules that turn one into the
other. The JSON namespace the templates read is specified in
{!page-"template-context"}; this page is only about wiring it to a build.

The example is real: it lives in [pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-site/] and
builds with

{v
dune build @pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-site/vault-site
v}

{1 The tree}

Everything sits in one directory — the templates alongside the notes they read:

{v
pkg/oystermark/examples/vault-site/
├── dune
├── focaccia.md
├── folding.svg
├── kneading.md
├── sourdough.md
├── starter.md
├── health.md.jinja
├── hydration.md.jinja
├── index.md.jinja
└── tags.md.jinja
v}

Building adds the generated notes beside them:

{v
├── health.md     ← health.md.jinja
├── hydration.md  ← hydration.md.jinja, via a code block in sourdough.md
├── index.md      ← index.md.jinja
└── tags.md       ← tags.md.jinja
v}

A vault does not have to be flat — [note.dir] and [note.segments] exist so a
template can group a nested vault by subtree — but a flat one keeps this
example about the wiring.

{1 A note}

Each note carries the frontmatter the templates filter and sort on:

{v
---
title: Sourdough Loaf
tags: [bread, weekend]
created: 2026-01-15
---
# Sourdough Loaf

A long, slow bake. Build the levain from [[starter]] the night before, then
follow the folds in [[kneading]].
v}

Only [tags] and [created] are read by name. A note with no [title] falls back
to its first heading, and [focaccia.md] writes its tags the inline way
([tags: bread, quick]) to show both forms are accepted.

{1 The rules}

One rule per generated note, each depending on the whole directory and on its
own template:

{v
(rule
 (targets index.md)
 (deps
  (source_tree .)
  index.md.jinja)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   index.md
   (pipe-stdout
    (run %{bin:oyster} context .)
    (run minijinja-cli --format=json index.md.jinja -)))))

(alias
 (name vault-site)
 (deps index.md tags.md health.md))
v}

Four parts of that stanza are worth spelling out.

{2 [pipe-stdout], not [pipe-stdin]}

The pipe is named for the output being redirected, so the action that connects
one command's stdout to the next command's stdin is [pipe-stdout]. There is no
[pipe-stdin] action.

{2 [(source_tree .)]}

A template that queries the whole vault depends on every note in it. The
dependency is therefore coarse on purpose: editing any note rebuilds every
generated page. Fine-grained dependencies would require knowing which notes a
template actually read, which the engine does not report.

It is also correct, which a narrower dependency would not be. Adding a link to
[focaccia.md] changes the count printed for [kneading.md], and the rule reruns.

Generated notes land in the same directory as their sources, and this does not
feed back: [dune] excludes every rule target from the source tree it hands the
action, so [index.md] is not a note in the context that produced it, nor in the
one that produces [tags.md]. The note count stays at four however many pages
are generated beside them.

One wrinkle, visible under [assets] rather than [notes]: when a rule writes its
target directly, as [(with-stdout-to context.json (run ... context .))] does,
the file is opened before the action runs, so the walk finds it — empty — and
[context.json] lists itself as an asset. It is never a note, so templates that
iterate [notes] are unaffected.

{2 A named alias}

[minijinja-cli] is not an OCaml dependency; [(run minijinja-cli ...)] resolves
it from [PATH]. Attaching the generated notes to their own alias rather than to
[@default] keeps a build that never renders from needing the binary at all.

{2 [%{bin:oyster}]}

Resolves the executable from the workspace, so the rule does not depend on
[oyster] being installed.

{1 Templates}

List every note newest first, annotated with its tags and how many notes point
at it:

{v
# Recipe index

{{ vault.note_count }} notes, newest first.

{% for n in notes | sort(attribute="created", reverse=true) -%}
- [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }})
{%- if n.tags %} — {{ n.tags | join(", ") }}{% endif %}
{%- if n.backlink_count %} · referenced by {{ n.backlink_count }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
v}

which produces

{v
# Recipe index

4 notes, newest first.

- [Sourdough Loaf](sourdough.md) — bread, weekend
- [Focaccia](focaccia.md) — bread, quick
- [Starter](starter.md) — reference · referenced by 1
- [Kneading](kneading.md) — technique, reference · referenced by 2
v}

Drive an index off the tag map instead, resolving each path through
[notes_by_path]:

{v
{% for tag, paths in tags | items -%}
## {{ tag }} ({{ paths | length }})

{% for p in paths -%}
- [{{ notes_by_path[p].title }}]({{ p }})
{% endfor %}
{% endfor -%}
v}

{v
## bread (2)

- [Focaccia](focaccia.md)
- [Sourdough Loaf](sourdough.md)

## quick (1)

- [Focaccia](focaccia.md)
...
v}

Or report on the graph rather than listing it:

{v
{{ vault.note_count }} notes · {{ vault.link_count }} links · {{ vault.unresolved_count }} unresolved

## Links pointing nowhere

{% for u in unresolved -%}
- `{{ u.source }}`:{{ u.line }} → `{{ u.target }}` ({{ u.reason }})
{% endfor %}
## Nothing links here

{% for p in orphans -%}
- [{{ notes_by_path[p].title }}]({{ p }})
{% endfor -%}
v}

{v
4 notes · 5 links · 1 unresolved

## Links pointing nowhere

- `starter.md`:7 → `hydration-table` (missing_path)

## Nothing links here

- [Focaccia](focaccia.md)
- [Sourdough Loaf](sourdough.md)
v}

{1 Filtering}

Selecting on a tag is a membership test on the note's [tags], not a
[selectattr] against a list:

{v
{% for n in notes | selectattr("created") | sort(attribute="created", reverse=true) %}
{% if "bread" in n.tags %}- {{ n.created }} — [{{ n.title }}]({{ n.path }})
{% endif %}{% endfor %}
v}

[selectattr("tags", "in", \["bread"\])] silently matches nothing: it tests
whether the whole [tags] list is an element of [\["bread"\]]. Sorting on
[created] works directly because the dates are ISO-8601 strings — see
{!page-"template-context".dates}.

{1 Executing a code block}

A note can own a computation as well as prose. [oyster block] queries the nodes
of a note and writes what it finds to stdout. Here the query selects the code
block carrying the [hydration] id and asserts its language at the same time;
[-print prop:text] writes the code without its fence, so the build system can
pipe it wherever it belongs — here, into [uv]:

{v
{#hydration}
```python
import json

flour, water, salt = 500, 350, 10
print(json.dumps({"hydration": {"flour": flour, "water": water, ...}}))
```
v}

{v
oyster block NOTE \
  '[Descendant(where=[Is(code_block), Prop(id, =, ID), Prop(lang, =, INFO)])]' \
  -print prop:text
v}

The language predicate is part of the selection: if the named block is not an
[INFO] code block, the query matches nothing. A query that matches nothing is
an error, so a rule fails rather than feeding an interpreter an empty string.
Nodes can also be addressed by heading, field, kind and position; repeated
[-print] flags can write their source, path, kind, line, file or properties.

Three rules: extract and run, snapshot the context, render the two together.
[minijinja-cli] accepts several data files and merges them, so the script's
output arrives as ordinary template variables:

{v
(rule
 (targets hydration.json)
 (deps sourdough.md)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   hydration.json
   (pipe-stdout
    (run
     %{bin:oyster}
     block
     sourdough.md
     "[Descendant(where=[Is(code_block), Prop(id, =, hydration), Prop(lang, =, python)])]"
     -print
     prop:text)
    (run uv run -)))))

(rule
 (targets context.json)
 (deps (source_tree .))
 (action
  (with-stdout-to context.json (run %{bin:oyster} context .))))

(rule
 (targets hydration.md)
 (deps hydration.md.jinja context.json hydration.json)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   hydration.md
   (run minijinja-cli --format=json hydration.md.jinja context.json
     hydration.json))))
v}

The template then reads [hydration] beside the vault's own namespace, and
editing the numbers in the note changes the rendered page on the next build.

{2 Addressing by id, not by position}

"The third Python block" is a reference into a document people edit: insert an
example above it and the build quietly runs something else. An id is stable
under editing, and it is the same anchor the rest of the vault already uses —
the LSP resolves it, renames it, and reports it when it breaks.

{2 [{#id}] is a Jinja comment}

Djot's attribute syntax collides with Jinja's comment delimiters: a template
containing a literal [{#hydration}] opens a comment that is never closed, and
the render fails with a syntax error. Quote it to write it out:

{v
Computed by running the {{ "{#hydration}" }} block in ...
v}

{2 What you are agreeing to}

The vault becomes trusted input: building it runs code that any note can
contain. And [dune] caches the result, so a script that reads the clock, the
network, or the environment makes the build depend on something the build
cannot see. Keep executed blocks deterministic, or accept that the page is a
snapshot of whenever it last ran.
